      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 12 and 20, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers, which is the largest number that divides both of them without leaving a remainder.\n\nHere are the steps for the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n1. Divide the larger number (20) by the smaller number (12) and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   20 \\div 12 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 20 - 12 \\times 1 = 8\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   20 = 12 \\times 1 + 8\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, replace the larger number (20) with the smaller number (12) and the smaller number with the remainder (8), and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   12 \\div 8 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 12 - 8 \\times 1 = 4\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   12 = 8 \\times 1 + 4\n   \\]\n\n3. Replace the larger number (12) with the smaller number (8) and the smaller number with the remainder (4), and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   8 \\div 4 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 8 - 4 \\times 2 = 0\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   8 = 4 \\times 2 + 0\n   \\]\n\n4. When the remainder becomes 0, the divisor at that step is the GCD. Therefore, the GCD of 12 and 20 is 4.\n\nSo, the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20 is \\boxed{4}.",
